Abstract
The continuous need for improving the efficiency of growing food for humans and animals has led to extensive use of antibiotics as essential medicines. However, due to the problems associated with their uncontrolled disposal, we are now facing serious environmental contamination by antibiotics and its associated health concerns. In an effort to resolve this problem, nanostructured electrochemical platforms comprising of carbonaceous nanoparticles, metal nanoparticles, magnetic nanoparticles, metal organic platforms and quantum dots have been proposed to detect antibiotic residues. Despite the significant progress, further efforts are still required to develop multi-functional nanomaterials and to ensure efficient portability within electrochemical sensors using communication devices. Therefore, the present review summarizes an in-depth evaluation of the nanoparticleassisted electrochemical sensing of antibiotics residues in diverse matrices (e.g., human fluids, the environmental, food, and beverages samples). The present review begins with a brief introduction to antibiotics followed by a survey on the existing electroanalytical techniques to highlight the significance of nanomaterial-based electrochemical sensing for antibiotics in diverse matrices.
Finally, the review provides an outlook on the future concepts of this research field by defining the limitations to improving the sensing performance for antibiotic detection.
