## Table of Contents
## 1. Introduction
Nanomaterials (NMs) have been introduced in the recent past, and they are now an integral part of most of the current analytical techniques for health, environment, food safety, and pharmaceutical analysis. Due to their high surface area and extraordinary physical/chemical/electronic properties, NMs can enhance the interaction with biomolecules compared to their bulk counterparts (Kerman et al., 2008; Wang, 2005). Further, novel bio-electronic sensing devices can be developed with signal transduction mechanisms to facilitate the recognition events due to the ease of modification with biological entities (e.g., antibodies, aptamers, enzymes, RNA, and DNA) ((Abi et al., 2018; Chen and Chatterjee, 2013; Li et al., 2009; Luo et al., 2006; Zhu et al., 2015). The factors controlling the performance of NMs include chemical composition, crystal structure quality, crystallographic axis orientation, morphology, and dimensions, as these can affect the extent of interaction and electron transfer rate (Chen and Chatterjee, 2013).
In light of their great potential in sensing applications, NMs have been employed exclusively for developing clinical and field-deployable analytical instruments for various monitoring applications (e.g., health, environment, biological process, and food safety) due to their advantages (e.g., costeffectiveness, high sensitivity, selectiveness, rapid response, and portability) (Maduraiveeran and Jin, 2017). Therefore, a wide variety of electrochemical sensors with diverse sensing principles (e.g., amperometric, potentiometric, and electrochemical impedance) have been developed to pursue practical applications. However, the performance of an electrochemical sensor significantly depends on the presence of a nanostructured surface that is precisely designed for selective interaction with the target analyte and for signal amplification (Kimmel et al., 2012). For instance, differential-pulse and square wave voltammetry has been specifically designed for continuous monitoring of environment pollutants (e.g., pesticides, hormones, antibiotics, and heavy metals) and clinically relevant analyte molecules.
The current knowledge on electrochemical sensing of antibiotics based on various nanomaterials has scantily been updated in the existing literature. This review was hence organized to highlight the application of nanomaterials towards electrochemical detection of antibiotics within diverse biomatrices. It is noted that nanomaterial-assisted electrochemical sensors have been constructed as a new platform for environment analysis (Chen et al., 2017; Lan et al., 2017; Xue et al., 2016). This review describes how different nanomaterials can be employed in designing high-performance electrochemical sensing platforms for efficient detection of antibiotics in diverse systems. The review also discusses the challenges encountered by the existing nanomaterials and how the performance of electrochemical sensors can be improved further. Therefore, the concepts introduced in this review are expected to motivate new findings towards nanomaterial assisted electrochemical detection of antibiotics in the future.
## 2. Antibiotics: A major environmental pollutant
Antibiotics emerged as revolutionary medicines in the health industry for treating a wide range of infectious diseases in both livestock and humans. Considered as “wonder drugs”, antibiotics are defined as chemotherapeutic agents that restrain or prevent the growth of microorganisms (Zaman et al., 2017). However, due to excessive use, a few microbes have become resistant towards specific antibiotics. Due to structural complexity, most antibiotics are excreted unaltered in urine and feces, ultimately contaminating soil and natural water resources (Carvalho and Santos, 2016; Thiele‐Bruhn, 2003). Recently, extensive usage of antibiotics in animal feed (for improving growth and production) has also contaminated the food products consumed by humans (Mungroo and Neethirajan, 2014).
The presence of antibiotics is usually determined by the persistence of the consumed antibiotics in the environment. The normal concentration of antibiotics usually lies within the range of ng to low µg per liter in environmental samples (Cristea et al. 2015). However, antibiotics residues, if present in excessive amounts, can pose serious threats by causing diseases like pneumonia, tuberculosis, and gonorrhea, making their treatment complicated. In addition to infections, antibiotic resistance has also been associated with severe allergic reactions in humans. If the same trend continues, it is estimated that 300 million people will die pre-maturely worldwide over the next 35 years (WHO Guidelines, 2015). World Health Organization has declared “antibiotic resistance” to be a serious economic and societal threat to public health (WHO Guidelines, 2011; WHO Guidelines, 2014). Therefore, a number of antibiotics (e.g., tetracycline, penicillin, fluoroquinolones, $\upbeta$ -lactams, and sulphonamides) have been identified as potential environmental contaminants (EMA Guidelines, 2015). These classes of antibiotics have been further selected for fast and sensitive detection in diverse matrices (e.g., food, beverages, and environmental samples). A brief list of such commonly used antibiotics as pollutants for human and veterinary systems has been selected and summarized in Table 1.
## 2.1. Mechanism of antibiotic toxicity, interaction, and resistance
Antibiotics usually inhibit the synthesis of bacterial cell walls, protein, DNA, RNA, and folic acid (Fig. 1) to combat infections or microbes (Levy and Marshall, 2004). Antibiotics have been explicitly developed to influence microorganism and bacterial populations in a dosage-dependent manner. The detrimental effect of antibiotics leads to the disappearance or elimination of microbial communities by bactericidal and bacteriostatic effects (Alanis, 2005). However, a few antibiotics are supposed to exert a selective force on microbial populations with resistance and genotypic and phenotypic
variability (Levy, 1992). Although several newer classes of antibiotics have emerged to target disease-causing microbes over the past few decades, their excessive and irresponsible usage often led to resistant strains of bacteria (Chopra et al., 2002).   
Antibiotic resistance is a defense mechanism employed by the microbial population to avoid the lethal actions of antibiotics. Some of the most common mechanisms for antibiotics can be accounted for by: i) the production of hydrolytic enzymes, ii) change in bacterial cell-wall permeability, and iii) changes in antibiotic target sites. All these processes will eventually help inhibit the entry of antibiotics within the microbial cell (Kapoor et al., 2017). To limit the occurrence of antibioticresistant microbes, dose adjustment and trace-level monitoring of antibiotics is highly desirable. To date, several analytical methods have been developed for accurate and reliable monitoring of antibiotic residues in natural resources. As compared to the existing analytical techniques, electrochemical biosensors have been demonstrated as portable, robust, fast response diagnostics for probing the residues of antibiotics with high sensitivity (Munteanu et al., 2018). Therefore, the next section deals with the operating principles of voltammetric electrochemical sensors with a discussion of the factors affecting the performance of an electrochemical sensor.
## 3. Electrochemical methodologies for the detection of antibiotic contaminants
In the recent decades, electrochemical sensors have shown enormous potential towards the detection of antibiotic residues due to their advantages such as excellent sensitivity, rapid analysis time, and possibility of miniaturization (through combinations between potential (E), current (I), charge (Q), and time (t)) (Labib et al., 2016). To date, voltammetric techniques (DPV, CV, SWV, and stripping voltammetry) have been employed most commonly for electrochemical sensing of antibiotic residues. However, chronoamperometry and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy have also gained considerable attention as an alternative option for the detection of antibiotics. A detailed description of these techniques has been made with respect to the detection principle, applications, and detection limit in Table 2.
## 3.2. Strategies for electrochemical detection of antibiotic contaminants
## 3.2.1. Receptor/enzyme-mediated electrochemical biosensor
The use of several receptors/enzymes labeled with nanomaterials has allowed electrochemical biosensors to detect diverse forms of antibiotics. In a majority of such reports, several enzymes (such as horseradish peroxidase [HRP], pencillinase [PCN], and glucose oxidase [GOx]) were successfully employed for electrochemical detection of antibiotics using colloidal gold tags, carbonaceous nanomaterials, and  magnetic nanoparticles (Faridah et al., 2012; Gonçalves et al., 2014; Kling et al., 2016).
## 3.2. 2. Immuno-complex based electrochemical biosensor
Electrochemical immunosensors based on specific antigen-antibody recognition offer high reliability, cost-effectiveness, and required sensitivities towards targets (e.g., drug, environmental pollutants, and veterinary residues in food) (Wilson and Hu, 2000). However, electron mediators need to be incorporated into the detection procedure to electrochemically exploit highly specific “antigen-antibody” interactions (Zhao et al., 2011). Moreover, electrochemical immunosensors usually lack desired sensitivity to detect very low concentrations of antibiotic residues. Therefore, significant efforts have been directed towards developing novel nanomaterials for efficient immobilization of the biomolecules on the electrode surface. A number of NMs have been recognized for their potential to carry out label-free immunosensing of antibiotics, such as carbonaceous nanomaterials (carbon nanotubes and graphene)(Wu et al., 2012; Yuan et al., 2018), nanoporous gold (Zhao et al., 2011), polymers (poly-pyrrole-N-Hydroxysuccinimide) (Giroud et al., 2009), and magnetic nanomaterials (Liu et al., 2016). Apart from this, several enzyme labels (Zacco et al., 2007), quantum dots (Liu et al., 2014), and dye molecules (Wu et al., 2014) have also been used with antigen-antibody complexes to improve the sensitivity towards antibiotic residues.
## 3.2.3. Aptamer-based electrochemical biosensor
In recent years, aptamer-based electrochemical biosensors have emerged as a robust detection approach for antibiotic residues. Compared to antibodies, aptamers exhibit enhanced physicochemical stability and longer shelf-life with reasonable cost, easy in-vitro synthesis, and simple modification procedures (Pavlov et al., 2004; Saberian-Borujeni et al., 2014; Tolle et al., 2015). The immobilization of aptasensors is conventionally achieved through two major techniques: i) direct modification of a bio-functionalized sensor surface with aptamers using appropriate linkers and ii) non-covalent modification of functionally activated surfaces with aptamers. Nanomaterials (e.g., carbon fibers and gold nanoparticles) are commonly incorporated for electrochemical detection of antibiotics to improve the sensitivity of aptasensors (He and Yan, 2018; Li et al., 2018). These aptasensors have been further advanced through the use of methylene blue, ferrocene, and heavy metal ions doped MOFs $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ) to prepare highly sensitive and distinctive signal tags for multiplexed antibiotic detection (Chen et al., 2016; Huang et al., 2018).
## 3.2.4. Molecular Imprinted Polymer (MIP)-based electrochemical biosensor
Molecular imprinted sensors have gained increasing attention due to the presence of specific receptors sites towards target analyte molecules. The fabrication process of MIPs employs sacrificial spacer methods involving polymerization of cross linkers and functional monomers with template molecules through covalent and non-covalent approaches (Lian et al., 2012). A wide variety of nanomaterials (including graphene oxide, Prussian blue catalytic polymer, magnetic nanomaterials, metal nanoparticles, and graphene) has been employed extensively for increasing sensitivity to the MIP-based electrochemical sensor for antibiotic residues (Li et al., 2012; Liu, B. et al., 2013; Liu, Y. et al., 2013; Wang et al., 2014; Wang et al., 2011; Yola et al., 2014).
## 3.3. Nanomaterials for electrochemical sensing of antibiotics
Owing to the unique physical and chemical properties, nanomaterials are considered as the most attractive option in designing and construction of “functional” transducers for electrochemical biosensing systems. The flexibility of these systems in terms of post-modification or functionalization has helped improve the analytical performance dramatically towards the detection of antibiotics. In light of such development, discussion was given to describe various nanomaterials employed for the electrochemical detection of antibiotics in the next section.
## 3.3.1. Carbon based nanomaterials
Carbon is an extremely interesting element because it can have various hybridization states including sp, $\mathrm{sp}^{2},\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ , and $\mathrm{sp}^{2}\mathrm{-sp}^{3}$ combinations (Gogotsi, 2015). Due to its inherent attributes (e.g., high chemical stability, aspect ratio, biocompatibility, high electrical conductivity, and robust mechanical strength), carbonaceous nanomaterials are recognized as promising options for diverse biosensing applications. The unique electrochemical properties of carbonaceous nanomaterials depend on the diverse synthesis procedures. Therefore, many morphological variants of carbons (such as zero to three dimensional scales) have been employed conveniently as “nanoprobes” for routine biosensing applications (Wang and Dai, 2015). The next section discusses the carbonaceous nanomaterials commonly employed for electrochemical biosensing of antibiotics in diverse matrices.
## 3.3.1. 1. Carbon nanotubes
Since their discovery, carbon nanotubes have drawn considerable attention as one of the most efficient  nanostructured carbonaceous materials for biosensing applications (Iijima, 1991). Carbon nanotubes provide scaffolds for efficient immobilization of biomolecules along with a unique combination of physical, chemical, and electrical properties to make them highly suitable for recognition of analytes, metabolites, and disease biomarkers (Tîlmaciu and Morris, 2015). Further, the availability of various functionalization strategies enabled them to realize their maximal potential by improving their functional and physicochemical properties (e.g., solubility). As such, functionalized CNTs have become a pre-requisite for facile fabrication of diverse nano-devices (Sun et al., 2002). There have been numerous reports on nanostructuring of electrodes with CNTs/CNTcomposites for electrochemical biosensors for antibiotic detection. These nanomaterials led to a remarkable enhancement in electrochemical performance with a wider detection range and low limit of detection towards commonly employed antibiotics (Gayen et al. 2016; Govindasamy et al. 2017; Hajian et al. 2015).
The application of vertically aligned CNT-based electrochemical sensors was proposed for the detection of levofloxacin in urine samples of humans (Moraes et al., 2013). The sensor showed a detection limit of $75.2\ \mathrm{nM}$ over a detection range $(1{-}10\ \upmu\mathbf{M})$ towards levofloxacin in urine. This demonstrates that vertically aligned MWCNTs should exhibit excellent electrocatalytic activity towards levofloxacin electrooxidation. Likewise, carboxyl functionalized multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNT) were employed for the detection of tetracycline (TET) through signal amplification of an electrochemical aptasensor (Zhou et al., 2012). Here, MWCNTs were shown to impart conductibility and biocompatibility, resulting in a detection limit (LOD) of $5\mathrm{nM}$ towards TET in spiked milk samples. The response of the sensor was tested in the presence of two structurally identical derivatives of tetracycline  (OTC and DOX) in the analysis of real (e.g., milk) samples. The electrochemical response towards TET was further improved by developing a nanocomposite aptasensor using CS-Prussian blue-graphene nanoparticles (CS-PB-GR) and multiwalled carbon nanotubes-chitosan (MWCNTs-CS) (Guo et al., 2015a). The excellent synergy between multiwalled carbon nanotubes and graphene resulted in a wide detection range $(0.0001{-}10^{3}\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and a detection limit of $5.6\mathrm{\pM}$ towards tetracycline in spiked milk samples. The sensor response was also evaluated for interferences (e.g., oxytetracycline, kanamycin, and gentamycin) as a pre-scanning method for TET detection in livestock products. Due to their excellent electronic properties, metal nanoparticles have been extensively employed in combination with MWCNTs for devising electrochemical sensing platforms for diverse antibiotics. In this respect, an electrochemical sensor based on nanocomposites of carboxyl functionalized MWCNTs and Ag nanoparticles (MWCNTs-Ag nanoparticles) was developed for the detection of adriamycin (ADM)(Zhang and Zhang, 2010). The modification of the electrode with such composites enabled an enhanced electrochemical response of ADM with a detection limit of $1.7~\mathrm{{nM}}$ . Using DPV as a transduction method, the electrochemical signals were found to be linear within a concentration range from 8.2–19.0 nM. Further, the sensor was demonstrated to have excellent stability, reproducibility, and specificity in the detection of calf thymus DNA. In another report, an electrochemical sensor was developed by using composites of gold nanoparticles and multiwalled carbon nanotubes (Au NPs/en/MWCNTs/Au) for the detection of valrubicin in blood and urine samples (Hajian et al. 2015). The work was reported as a “stepping stone” towards the analytical determination of valrubicin after its discovery in 1998. The work also described variable interactions of DNA with valrubicin through efficient immobilization of different sequences of single-stranded DNA probes on the composite surface. Accordingly, cytosine and guanine sequences possessed more affinity towards valrubicin. The sensor exhibited high sensitivity towards valrubicin with a detection limit of $0.018~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (over a detection range of $0.5{-}80.0~\upmu\mathrm{M},$ ). The interference effects of some commonly observed species (e.g., sucrose, urea, glucose, $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ , and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ) were then assessed during the electrochemical response of valrubicin at the Au NPs/en/MWCNTs/Au based sensor in human urine and serum samples.
Further, multiwalled carbon nanotubes modified with gold nanoparticles (MWNTs-GNPs) were incorporated into molecularly imprinted polymer (MIP) to construct a tetracycline sensor (Wang et al., 2011). Here, the composite electrode was designed to provide a good number of imprinted sites with enhanced electron transfer ability (e.g., from the imprinted sites to the electrode surface). Using CV as a transduction method, the sensor revealed a low DL of $90~\mathrm{{nM}}$ towards tetracycline, which was attributed mainly to the excellent electrocatalytic activity of gold nanoparticles. The selectivity of the sensor was evaluated in the presence of common interferents (e.g., nafcillin, oxytetracycline, and chloramphenicol), and the authors of that study found that oxytetracycline results in more interference than nafcillin or chloramphenicol during TET detection. Later, an electrochemical sensor imprinted with neomycin was proposed based on graphene-multiwalled carbon nanotube/chitosansilver nanoparticle composites (Lian et al., 2013). Here, neomycin was used as a template molecule, while pyrrole served as a functional monomer. The authors discussed various parameters (e.g., template/functional monomer ratio, elution time, and incubation time) that influence the activity of the imprinted sensor towards neomycin. The sensor exhibited high binding affinity and sensitivity (limit of detection of $7.63~\mathrm{nM}$ ) due to combined effects of GR-MWCNTs and CS-SNP. Importantly, the sensor response remained unaffected in the presence of neomycin analogues and polycyclic compounds (e.g., gentamycin sulphate, streptomycin, kanamycin sulfate, and neomycin), which made it a promising cost-effective candidate for monitoring neomycin in real honey and milk samples.
A mixture of bimetallic Au-Pt nanoparticles was electrodeposited on multiwalled carbon nanotubes to construct a sensitive voltammetric system to detect cefotaxime (Shahrokhian and Rastgar, 2012). The composite had a detection limit of $1.0~\mathrm{nM}$ over a wide linear range (0.004–10 ${\upmu\mathbf{M}},$ , which was significantly higher than glassy carbon electrodes individually coated with MWCNT or Au–PtNPs. The considerable increase in electrochemical response was attributed to the effective electrochemical area of the MWCNT/Au-PtNPs modifier along with effective accumulation of CFX at the electrode surface. The modified electrode exhibited a linear dynamic range across three orders of magnitude in addition with other remarkable electrochemical advantages (e.g., reproducibility, repeatability, and anti-fouling behavior). As such, its feasibility was validated for CFX detection in pharmaceutical/clinical preparations.
A new sensor approach based on 3D imprinted nanohybrid structures was designed by using composites of 3DCNTs@Cu NPs. This sensor was developed using molecularly imprinted polymers (MIP) to detect chloramphenicol (Fig. 2) (Munawar et al., 2018). The sensor exhibited superior sensitivity towards chloramphenicol with a DL of $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . However, with this sensor design, $100\%$ selectivity was observed for chloramphenicol in the presence of interfering components (e.g., florfenicol, dansyl chloride, clindamycin, and thiamphenicol). It was assumed that this enhanced sensitivity of the sensor should be ascribable to the high conductivity of CNTs synergistically combined together with the catalytic activity of Cu NPs and specificity of the MIP network. A “mercury free” electrochemical sensing approach was developed towards trimethoprim (TMP) and sulfamethoxazole (SMX) antibiotics using MWCNT-SbNPs composites (Cesarino et al., 2013). They claimed that the addition of Sb nanoparticles onto MWCNTs had a synergistic effect on the electrochemical response towards concurrent analysis of SMX and TMP in environmental samples. The sensor provided a promising setup for simultaneous detection of SMX and TMP with detection limits of $24~\mathrm{nM}$ and $31\mathrm{nM}$ , respectively. Further, these authors tested the performance of the sensor toward  both targets in organic wastewater (endocrine disruptors and pesticides). Accordingly, they found that the paraffin/MWCNT-SbNPs composite electrode exhibited acceptable recoveries (97.2 – $101.6\%$ ) for analysis in natural water resources under optimized conditions. In light of the enormous potential of multiwalled carbon nanotubes and their composites with metal nanoparticles, an electrochemical sensor combining the CTAB surfactant and conducting polymer poly(diphenylamine) with multiwalled carbon nanotubes was developed to detect chloramphenicol (CAP) levels in biological samples (Kor and Zarei, 2014). These authors studied the mechanism of CAP reduction at the CTAB-PDPA- MWCNT modified electrode using cyclic voltammetry and showed that the process was adsorption controlled. They examined the effects of various parameters (e.g., accumulation conditions, scan rate, electrolyte solution, and amounts of MWCNT and CTAB) to offer stability, good selectivity, and a wide linear range towards CAP detection.
## 3.3.1. 2. Graphene-based nanomaterials
The emergence of graphene as a 2D carbon nanomaterial has drawn considerable attention in the electronics and sensor communities. A single graphene nanosheet is reported to exhibit 60 times more conductivity, larger surface area, stability, and better sensitivity as compared to individual single-walled carbon nanotubes (Kuila et al., 2011; Pumera, 2011). The presence of  good electron transfer and large specific surface area makes graphene an attractive candidate for electrochemical sensing of antibiotics (Wu et al. 2014b; Yuan et al. 2018). The excellent electrocatalytic activity of screen-printed carbon electrodes modified with graphene was explored for the detection of tetracycline (Filik et al., 2016). The sensor was promising for tetracycline detection in honey and milk samples at $0.08~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ levels with high precision. To further improve the devices, N-doped graphene nanosheet-Au NP composite (Au/N-G) sensors were proposed for the sensitive sensing of chloramphenicol (Borowiec et al., 2013). The sensor showed a DL of $0.59\ \upmu\mathrm{M}$ over a wide linear range (2.0 to $80.0\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . Detailed LSV studies suggested that Au/N-G efficiently generated an electrochemical response towards chloramphenicol. This response was tested in the presence several potential interferences (e.g., nitrobenzene, glucose, uric acid, ascorbic acid, metronidazole, chlorotetracycline, oxytetracycline, and $\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8,}$ ). Accordingly, it was promising for real sample analysis in chloramphenicol eye drops. A label-free electrochemical immunosensor was built for sensing kanamycin based on biocompatible water-soluble graphene sheet/Prussian blue chitosan/nanoporous gold (WGS/(PB-CTS)/(NPG) composite films (Zhao et al., 2011). The sensor adopted PB as an electron transfer mediator that is coupled with $-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{^{-}}$ terminated WGS through electrostatic adsorption. The excellent conductivity of WGS and NPG together facilitated e-transfer to realize an ultra-low detection limit of $0.01\ \mathrm{nM}$ towards kanamycin. Apart from high sensitivity, this sensor was  not significantly affected (e.g., less than $5\%$ ) by potential interferences (e.g., gentamicin, neomycin, and neamine antibiotics). Later, Qin and co-workers significantly improved the electrochemical response using a novel aptasensor modified with hierarchical nanoporous (HNP) Pt-Cu alloy and thionine functionalized graphene (GR-TH) as  biosensing substrates (Qin et al., 2016). The aptamer specific to kanamycin was assembled on HNP-PtCu/GR-TH/GCE using highaffinity interactions between $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group present over aptamer and Pt nanoparticles (Fig. 3). The aptasensor showed a wide linear range response within the concentration range from 0.001–100 nM and an ultra-low detection limit $(0.86~\mathrm{pM})$ towards kanamycin. In addition to high sensitivity, the sensor exhibited high selectivity towards kanamycin in the presence of interfering species (e.g., HCG, DA, and Tyr), suggesting its practical applicability for monitoring animal derived food.
A penicillin biosensor was proposed based on layer by layer assembled films containing graphene nanosheets adsorbed with ionic liquids/hematein/penicillinase (Wu et al. 2014b). The detection principle of the sensor was to employ penicillinase enzyme so that penicillin is catalytically converted into penicilloic acid and to monitor liberated $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ using hematein as a pH indicator. The sensor was capable of ultralow detection of penicillin $\mathrm{\Phi(DL}$ of $0.1\mathrm{\pM}\mathrm{,}$ ). Furthermore, the sensor exhibited acceptable accuracy to measure penicillin in real milk samples without being affected by potential interferences (e.g., ampicillin, kanamycin, streptomycin, and levofloxacin antibiotics). Here, the authors attributed the excellent electrochemical response to the film forming ability and high ionic conductivity of IL, which maintains favorable orientation of immobilized penicillinase enzyme on the electrode surface. Likewise, a highly sensitive levofloxacin (LEV) sensor was built by leveraging the selectivity of molecularly imprinted polypyrrole (MIP)(Wang et al., 2014). GrapheneAu composites were incorporated with MIP to promote high electrocatalytic activity towards electrooxidation of LEV. Before the synthesis of the graphene-Au composite, the surface of graphene was modified with poly(di allyl dimethylammonium chloride) (PDDA) to improve the solubility of graphene and facilitate further functionalization with Au NPs to construct an efficient sensor for LEV detection. Accordingly, the sensor demonstrated a wide linear range $(1.0\mathrm{-}100.0\upmu\mathrm{{M})}$ and a detection limit of $0.53\upmu\mathrm{M}$ towards LEV using a DPV transduction technique. The sensor was found to exhibit high sensitivity, selectivity, and good reproducibility towards LEV detection in LEV capsules.
Recently, a green and template–free approach was employed to develop 3-D reduced graphene oxide architectures  (Zhang et al., 2016). It was observed that the highly porous nature of 3-
DRGO/GCE imparted a better electrochemical response (LOD of $0.15~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ as compared with the existing metal/polymer based electrochemical sensors reported previously for CAP detection (Ganjali et al. 2012; Karthik et al. 2016). Further, a 3-D nanostructured composite (Au/carbon nitride/graphene) was also synthesized for increasing the active sites and electrical conductivity towards simultaneous determination of chloramphenicol and ciprofloxacin (Yuan et al., 2018). These authors claimed excellent electrochemical response towards CAP with a Dl of $27{\mathrm{~nM}}$ in the presence of ciprofloxacin. In addition, the sensor was also found to be promising for quantitative determination of chloramphenicol in milk samples with acceptable recoveries ranging from $95.0{-}101.2\%$ .
In addition, inherent unique chemical and physical properties of  metal oxides have made them promising for diverse biosensing applications in recent years. For instance, hydrothermally synthesized composites of cobalt oxide nanocrystals and reduced graphene oxide $(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{\Omega}$ rGO) were used to fabricate an electrochemical sensor for reliable  detection of chloramphenicol (CAP) (Yadav et al., 2019). The sensor exhibited a low limit of detection $(0.55\upmu\mathrm{M})$ with high sensitivity $(1.32\upmu\mathrm{A}\upmu\mathrm{M}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ through the electrocatalytic reduction of CAP by $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -rGO/GCE. To further improve the performance, an electrochemical sensor was constructed through modification of graphene oxide (GO) with 3D hierarchical zinc oxide $(Z\mathrm{nO})$ for efficient electrochemical sensing of chloramphenicol (CAP) (Sebastian et al., 2019). As compared to previous reports, the application of GO/ZnO nanomaterials significantly improved the detection limit $(0.01~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and also enabled high sensitivity $(7.27\ \upmu\mathrm{A}\ \upmu\mathrm{M}^{-1}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ towards CAP over a wide linear dynamic range for CAP (0.2–7.2 $\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . These authors also tested the response of the sensor towards biologically co-existing samples like ascorbic acid (AA), uric acid (UA), and glucose (Glu). Accordingly, they observed that the GO/ZnO modified electrode was suitable for practical applications. In a subsequent paper, heteroatom doping of graphene materials was proposed as a useful means to modulate the electrochemical properties (Wang et al., 2019). Therefore, an electrochemical sensor was built using a novel electrode based on chlorine doped reduced graphene oxide (Cl-RGO) for veterinary drug chloramphenicol contained in water, milk, calf plasma, and pharmaceutical samples. These authors reported that upgraded electrocatalytic activity and electrochemical conductivity of Cl-RGO facilitated the sensitive determination of the veterinary drug chloramphenicol. The sensor was demonstrated to have a linear relationship between current intensity and chloramphenicol concentration with a detection limit of $1~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ $\mathrm{\S}/\mathrm{N}=3\$ ) over a wide detection range $(2{-}35~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ using the DPV transduction method. Further, the sensor was effectively free from interferences from antibiotics such as penicillin G, erythromycin, and tetracycline.
A novel strategy involving composite films of graphene quantum dots with poly (o-aminophenol) (PoAP/GQD) was introduced for the voltammetric determination of levofloxacin (Huang et al., 2017). The composite film was found to exhibit a strong electrocatalytic activity towards levofloxacin to yield a detection limit of $10\mathrm{nM}$ within the concentration range of 0.05 to $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Further, the sensor was also found to exhibit excellent selectivity and standard recoveries $(96.0\ –101.0\%$ ), making its suitable for levofloxacin detection in milk samples. A promising biomimetic platform was also developed using composites of electrochemically reduced graphene oxide (PPy3C/ERGO) and streptomycin (STR) imprinted poly(pyrrole-3-carboxy acid) $(\mathrm{PPy}_{3}\mathrm{C})$ towards trace level detection of streptomycin (Wen et al., 2017). The sensor was demonstrated to have a low limit of detection (0.5 nM), reliable practicability, and good molecular recognition towards STR determination in food samples such as honey and porcine kidney samples. Further, composites of $\upbeta$ -cyclodextrin and reduced–graphene oxide $\upbeta$ -CD/r-GO) were tested to electrochemically detect gatifloxacin (Jiang et al., 2016). To ensure maximal electrochemical response towards gatifloxacin, detailed optimization studies were carried out in terms of $\mathrm{pH}$ of the supporting electrolyte, electro-polymerization cycles, and accumulation potential. The electrochemical sensor showed a low detection limit $(0.02\upmu\mathrm{M})$ with a sensitivity of $0.33\ \upmu\mathrm{A}\ \upmu\mathrm{M}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ over a wide linear detection range $(0.05{-}150\ \upmu\mathrm{M})$ towards gatifloxacin. Moreover, the sensor was also found to exhibit enhanced selectivity, reproducibility, and long-term stability, making it useful for real applications in human urine samples.
A novel electrochemical sensor using electrochemically reduced graphene was recently explored for the sensitive determination of midecamycin (MD) (Xi and Ming 2012). The modification of the electrode with reduced graphene facilitated electrochemical determination of MD relative to bare GCE. Further, the sensor recorded a wide detection range $0.3\mathrm{-}200~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ with a detection limit of 0.1 $\upmu\mathrm{M}$ towards MD. Also, the sensor held satisfactory recoveries to undergo real sample analysis of MD in biological fluids as well as pharmaceutical formulations. In the continuation of that work, a novel approach involving the synergistic combination of metal oxides and ionic liquids with graphene oxide (NiO/graphene oxide nanocomposite-ionic liquids) was also developed for trace level detection of sulphamethoxazole (SMTZ) (Shabani-Nooshabadi and Roostaee, 2016). Detailed electrochemical investigations suggested an excellent electrocatalytic activity and cumulative effect of the nanocomposites towards detection of SMTZ in urine and tablet samples.
## 3.3.1. 3. Other nanostructured carbon materials
Due to the biocompatibility and good adsorption ability of carbonaceous nanomaterials, significant efforts have been directed towards developing novel types of these materials for improving the detection limit and performance of electrochemical sensor towards antibiotics detection. In this respect, porous carbon nanorods and multifunctional graphene nanocomposites ${\mathrm{\bfGR-Fe}}_{3}{\mathrm{\bfO}}_{4}.$ -Au NPs/PCNR) were employed for sensitive electrochemical detection of streptomycin (Yin et al., 2017).
The authors claimed that the presence of GR-Fe ${}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -Au NP as amplification labels imparted an enhanced detection limit of $0.04{\mathrm{~nM}}$ towards streptomycin. Using DPV as a transduction method, the aptasensor displayed a wide detection range $\mathbf{0.08{-}340\ n M}$ , great reproducibility, high sensitivity and good selectivity, suggesting its suitability for STR detection in milk samples. Later, composites of carbon fiber and mesoporous carbon-gold nanoparticles (OMC-Au NPs/CNFs) were also employed for electrochemical sensing of streptomycin (STR) along with kanamycin (KAN) (Li et al., 2018). High sensitivity was observed toward STR and KAN with limits of detection (LODs) of 45.0 and $87.3\ \mathrm{\pM}$ , due to the presence of cadmium sulfide (CdS) and lead sulfide (PbS) as amplification labels, respectively. Using DPV as a transduction method, the sensor demonstrated a linear response over 0.1–1,000 nM for both STR and KAN antibiotics. The excellent electrochemical response of the aptasensor was attributed to the unique synergy between CNFs and OMC-Au NPs, which imparted high electronic conductivity and improved electron transfer kinetics towards the sensor performance. Further, nanodiamond was introduced as a sensitive carbonaceous nanomaterial for undergoing label-free  electrochemical sensing of pyrazinamide (Simioni et al., 2017). The sensor showed a detection limit of $220~\mathrm{nM}$ over a wide linear range $(0.7\mathrm{-}49~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ towards pyrazinamide without requiring expensive signal tags or labels.
In a subsequent work, nanocomposites consisting of iron oxide and mesoporous carbon $(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{mC_{x}}$ , $\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{\partial}$ calcination temperature) were obtained from calcination of Fe (II) based metalorganic frameworks (525-MOF) to undergo label-free electrochemical detection of oxytetracycline (Song et al., 2017). Due to strong bioaffinity, chemical functionality, and high stability, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}@\mathrm{mC}_{900}$ based nanocomposites were successfully explored as an efficient immobilization platform for oxytetracycline (OTC) aptamer strands with an ultra-low detection limit $(0.00005~\mathrm{nM})$ towards tetracycline. Impedimetric studies were used to demonstrate a wide detection range (0.01–
$2.17\ \mathrm{\nM}$ for oxytetracycline. The sensor showed excellent anti-interference capability in the presence of tetracycline, doxycycline, and chlortetracycline to undergo OTC detection in milk samples. Likewise, nanocomposites of mesoporous carbon nanospheres embedded with SnOx $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{X}\ =1$ , 2, 3) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystals $(\mathrm{SnOx}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{mC})$ were conveniently employed to undergo label-free electrochemical aptasensing of tobramycin (TOB) antibiotics (Wang et al., 2018). Here, the nanocomposites were specifically designed to impart high surface area, good electrochemical activity, and high affinity towards TOB targeted aptamers to achieve a detection limit of 0.01nM. Using EIS as transduction method, the sensor exhibited wide detection range $(0.02\mathrm{-}10\mathrm{nM})$ ), excellent sensitivity, and selectivity to realize its practical applicability for TOB detection in serum and human urine samples. In one of the very first investigations (Deroco et al., 2018), an important antibiotic amoxicillin (AMX) was simultaneously detected in the presence of an inflammatory drug (nimesulide-NIM) using a cost-effective carbon black (CB)-dihexadecylphosphate (DHP) composite. The sensor revealed a detection limit of $0.12\upmu\mathrm{M}$ towards AMX using a square wave voltammetric method. Further, the simple concept of a ratiometric electrochemical aptasensor was introduced for tetracycline detection (Xu et al., 2017). Here, the authors integrated two separate aptamers (functionalized with ferrocene $(\mathrm{Fc})$ and Au NPs composites and CNFs and Au NPs composites, respectively) to immobilize anti-TET aptamers. The sensor was effective in detecting TET in the concentration range of $0.00002{\scriptstyle-2.2}~\upmu\mathbf{M}$ with a DL of $0.6\ \mathrm{nM}$ . Moreover, the sensor was also a successful option in solving the problem of low accuracy and large differences between batches.
A simple, reliable, and semi-derivative method was developed utilizing signal enhancement of yolk-shell structured carbon sphere ${\ @\mathbf{M}}\mathrm{nO}_{2}$ based composites $(\mathbf{CS}@\mathbf{MnO}_{2} $ ) for detection of chlortetracycline residues (Gan et al., 2015). Here, the authors achieved a unique yolk-shell structure using a facile redox reaction between CS and $\mathrm{{MnO}_{4}^{-}}$ in acidic conditions. Semi-derivative DPVs on $\mathbf{CS}@\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ modified GCEs showed a DL of $0.26\upmu\mathrm{M}$ towards TET (linear range: $0.5{-}300~\upmu\mathrm{M},$ ) due to the excellent accumulation ability and low density of the composite material. The sensor was promising for chlortetracycline detection in food samples with high tolerances towards (1) 3,000-fold concentrations of ${\mathrm{Na}}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{~K~}^{+}$ , (2) 1,500–fold concentrations of ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ , glucose, and glycine, and (3) 5- fold concentrations of oxytetracycline, rifampicin, and tetracycline. A label-free sensor for chloramphenicol was also developed using effective modification of glassy carbon electrodes with iron-nitrogen co-doped ordered mesoporous carbon-silicon nanocomposites (Si-Fe/NOMC) (Yalikun et al., 2018). Due to the excellent electrocatalytic properties of Si-Fe/NOMC composites, the sensor was efficient in assaying chloramphenicol over a $1{-}500~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ range with a detection limit of $0.03\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Further, this sensor was employed successfully in determining chloramphenicol in eye drop samples due to excellent selectivity, stability, and reproducibility.
## 3.3.2. Metal nanoparticles
Metal nanoparticles offer exclusive physical, chemical, and electronic properties that make them suitable as a transducer component of an electrochemical biosensor. Further, excellent catalytic and conductive properties allowed them to act as "electronic wires"  while promoting e-transfer between the electrode surface and redox centers in proteins (Luo et al. 2006). Metal nanoparticles are considered promising as an efficient immobilization matrix for aptamers, proteins, antibodies, and enzymes (Zhu et al. 2015). Therefore, the next section of this review highlights the recent advances in metal nanomaterials in the field of electrochemical detection of antibiotics.
Gold nanoparticles (Au NPs) possess distinct and highly favorable physicochemical properties (e.g., high conductivity, large surface area, good biocompatibility, and electrocatalysis characteristics)
for diverse sensing applications in chemical and biological samples (Zeng et al. 2011). Among all metal nanoparticles, Au NPs have been widely explored for improving the detection limits in electrochemical biosensing studies (Pingarrón et al. 2008; Yáñez-Sedeño and Pingarrón 2005). Further, the excellent sensitivity of AuNPs has been commonly combined with bio-recognition elements (e.g., aptamers, enzymes, and antibodies) to achieve diverse electrochemical sensing platforms for antibiotic detection. In this regard, a label-free electrochemical aptasensing approach was constructed to undergo highly sensitive and selective detection of daunomycin (Chandra et al., 2011). Here, both phosphatidylserine (PS) and aptamers were efficiently co-immobilized (specific receptors) on the surface of AuNPs functionalized with a conducting polymer like [2, 2': 5'2"- terthiophene-3’ (p-benzoic acid)](polyTTBA). Further, detailed optimization studies (pH, concentration of immobilized aptamer, temperature, PS:aptamer ratio, and reaction times) were carried out to obtain a DL of $0.052~\mathrm{{nM}}$ towards daunomycin over a wide linear range $\left(0.1{-}60~\mathrm{nM}\right)$ . The sensor showed excellent stability (7 weeks), sensitivity, and selectivity towards sensing of daunomycin in human urine. Likewise, another label-free electrochemical aptasensing approach was conveniently employed towards detection of kanamycin (Zhu et al. 2012). Here, an in-vitro selected kanamycin specific DNA aptamer was covalently immobilized onto gold nanoparticles (Au NPs) functionalized with poly–[2,5-di(2-thienyl)-1H-pyrrole-1- (p-benzoic acid)](Poly-DPB). This aptasensor showed a detection limit of $9.4\mathrm{nM}$ (linear range: $0.05\mathrm{~-~}9.0~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ towards kanamycin using LSV as a transduction method. Further, high sensitivity of the aptasensor $(4.26\times10^{-3}{\upmu\mathrm{A}}\ {\upmu\mathrm{M}}^{-1}\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{-2})$ was attributed to the enhanced conductivity of a self-assembled poly-DPB (Au NP) based sensing platform, which facilitated e-transfer for the signal transducer. The electrochemical performance towards kanamycin was further improved by exploring the biomimetic peroxidase-like activity of Au NPs in combination with target-induced replacement of a kanamycin-specific aptamer (Wang et al.
2016). These authors reported it as an enzyme–free approach with a wide detection range (0.1 - 60 nM) and ultra-low detection limit $(0.06~\mathrm{nM})$ towards kanamycin. The sensor was effectively free from potential interferences including aminoglycoside antibiotics (e.g., gentamycin, streptomycin, and neomycin) and non-aminoglycoside antibiotics (e.g., tetracycline), suggesting its applicability for food and environmental monitoring.
Moreover, molecularly imprinted polymer (MIP) was also used to propose a novel concept of support-less electrochemical platform for ultra-sensitive detection of metronidazole (MNZ)(Song et al., 2016). Here, electropolymerized molecularly imprinted polymer (MIP) was modified with porous Au-Ag microrods to undergo simultaneous identification and quantification of MNZ. Due to the unique combination of free-standing and nanoporous microelectrodes with MIP coatings, the sensor exhibited an ultra-low DL of $0.02\ \mathrm{pM}$ towards MNZ. Moreover, the sensor demonstrated excellent selectivity towards common analogs of MNZ and analytes like glucose, $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , Cl-, and ${\bf N O}_{3}{\bf\bar{\Psi}}$ . A label free novel electrochemical sensing approach was also proposed for tetracycline by Guo and co-workers(Guo et al., 2015b). Here, the authors modified glassy carbon electrode with electrodeposited Au NPs and methylene blue (MB) to ensure efficient immobilization of antitetracycline aptamer through Au-S bonds. The modified electrode recorded a remarkable voltammetric response due to combined attributes of constituent materials including enhanced etransfer ability of gold nanoparticles and maximized adsorption of tetracycline using MB. Therefore, CV response showed a linear calibration plot $(0.0001{-}1,000\upmu\mathrm{M})$ with a DL of $4.2\ \mathrm{pM}$ towards tetracycline. Further, the sensor was also investigated for stability, reproducibility, and selectivity against structural analogues of tetracycline (e.g., oxytetracycline, gentamycine sulphate, and kanamycin monosulphate) during tetracycline detection in milk samples.
The feasibility of polyhedral shaped gold nanocubes (synthesized with seed-mediated approach) as electrochemically detective amplifiers for label-free electrochemical aptasensing of chloramphenicol (CAP) was explored (BagheriHashkavayi et al. 2015). These authors utilized cysteine to prepare aptamer/AuNCs-Cys interdigitated screen printed electrodes for selective CAP detection. Upon optimization, the aptasensor demonstrated two linear detection ranges $(0.03\mathrm{-}0.10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ ; and 0.25– 6.0 $\upmu\mathrm{M})$ with a detection limit of $4{\mathrm{~nM}}$ towards CAP. Further, the sensor also held additional advantages of good selectivity and stability to be applied for CAP detection in human serum samples. Later, another cost-effective electrochemical aptasensor was developed by Hashkavayi and co-workers using dendritic gold nanostructures for chloramphenicol (CAP) (Hashkavayi et al., 2016). Here, the system included dendritic gold nanostructures along with a molecular recognition element, CAP binding aptamer,  and 1,4 diazabicyclo [2.2.2] octane (DABCO) supported mesoporous silica (SBA15) on a screen-printed electrode. Using DPV as a transduction method, the sensor demonstrated two linear ranges $(0.03\mathrm{-}0.15\upmu\mathrm{M}$ ; $0.15{-}7.0~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ for CAP with a DL of $4\mathrm{nM}$ . The sensor was recognized for enhanced sensitivity, stability, selectivity, and repeatability to offer a promising platform for CAP detection in human serum samples. Hashkavayi and co-workers (2016) made two consecutive gold nanomaterial based label-free electrochemical aptasensors for CAP. These authors then laid down the foundation of a structure switching label-free electrochemical aptasensor to selectively detect epirubicin (EPI) antibiotics (Hashkavayi and Raoof 2017). Here, nanocomposites consisting of gold nanoparticles electrodeposited on magnetic double charged diazoniabicyclo [2.2.2] octane dichlorosilica hybrid Au NP/Fe3O4 $@$ SiO2/DABCO were employed for effective immobilization of 5’thiolterminated aptamer (specific to epirubicin) to achieve a low detection limit $(0.04\upmu\mathrm{M})$ towards EPI detection. The sensor showed excellent sensitivity, selectivity (Paclitaxel, Docetaxel, Tamoxifen, and Imatinib), and two linear detection ranges $(0.07{-}1.0~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and $1.0{-}21.0\ \upmu\mathrm{M})$ to support EPI detection in human serum samples.
Apart from the successful application of aptamers, enzymes have also been used in combination with gold nanomaterials to impart necessary sensitivity and selectivity towards electrochemical detection of antibiotics (del Torno-de Román et al. 2016). In this respect, tyrosinase crosslinked gold nanoparticle modified screen-printed electrodes were used as a disposable amperometric biosensor for the assay of sulphamethoxazole. The biosensor exhibited a linear detection over $0.02\mathrm{-}0.2\ \mathrm{mM}$ with a DL of $22.6~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ towards sulphamethoxazole. Later, Chen and co-workers reported a novel signal amplification strategy using horseradish peroxidase functionalized gold nanoprobes for electrochemical aptasensing of kanamycin (Fig. 4) (Chen et al., 2018). It was observed that a combination of enzyme based catalytic reactions and nanoprobe assisted signal amplification enabled trace level detection of kanamycin. The presence of HRP assisted the electrocatalytic reaction in combination with  multi-label signal amplification of the nanoprobe, resulting in a wide detection range $(0.00004{\-}0.2\ \upmu\mathbf{M})$ and ultra–low detection limit $(1.8~\mathrm{pM})$ . In addition, the aptasensor tolerated huge interferences in the presence of diverse potential interfering antibiotics (e.g., chloramphenicol, thiamphenicol, florfenicol, streptomycin, and gentamycin), confirming its ability to analyze kanamycin in milk samples. Further, diverse metal nanoparticles have been explored for performing direct electrocatalytic determination of antibiotics due to their high surface area and excellent catalytic efficiencies. Therefore, gold colloids were electrodeposited on the tungsten tip of microelectrodes to develop a novel direct electrochemical method for tetracycline (TC) determination (Wang et al., 2012). The oxidation peak currents were found to be linearly dependent on the TC concentrations with a DL of $0.2\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (range: $2.2\mathrm{-}22\upmu\mathrm{M}$ ). Likewise, gold nanoparticles were modified with a multi-walled carbon paste electrode (GNPs/MWCPE) to develop an electrochemical sensor to sense cefixime (Afkhami et al., 2013). The voltammetric response was thoroughly investigated (in terms of pH, accumulation potential, scan rate, and time) towards achieving maximal electrochemical response towards oxidation of cefixime. The oxidation process of cefixime was irreversible, while its adsorption was controlled at GNPs/MWCPE.
Due to the combined merits of carbon paste electrode and Au NPs, excellent analytical performance of GNPs/MWCPE was witnessed in terms of low detection limit $(3.0\ \mathrm{nM}$ : range of $0.01{-}200\upmu\mathrm{M})$ , high sensitivity, reproducibility, and repeatability over other existing reports in the literature. Further, the electrochemical response was also found to be unaffected by the presence of potential interfering species (such as uric acid, ascorbic acid, citric acid, oxalic acid, glucose, caffeine, $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ , and $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ ). Nanometer sized Au NPs were explored for efficient modification of magnetite nanostructures stabilized with carboxymethyl cellulose $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ‐CMC) to develop a synergistically amplified electrochemical sensing platform for chloramphenicol detection (Jakubec et al. 2016). Square wave voltammetric studies revealed a perfect linear relationship within the concentration range of $2.5\mathrm{-}25\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and a detection limit of $0.06\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . The sensor exhibited a recovery value of around $97\%$ to authenticate its applicability for chloramphenicol detection in human urine samples. Further, composites of silver $@$ gold nanoparticles and 5-(5-bromo-2- hydroxybenzylidenamino)-2- mercaptobenzimidazole IL assisted in developing an ultrasensitive ceftizoxime imprinted electrochemical sensor (Beytur et al., 2018). Detailed electrochemical studies revealed a wide linear detection range $\left(1{-}0.01\ \mathrm{nM}\right)$ ), high specificity, excellent repeatability, stability, and a detection limit of $2.0\mathrm{pM}$ towards ceftizoxime.
Apart from Au NPs, Pt nanoparticles were combined with MWCNTs (Pt-MWCNTs) to develop a voltammetric sensor of ceftriaxone in the presence of lidocaine (Shahrokhian et al., 2014). The sensor exhibited a detection limit of $9.01\ \mathrm{nM}$ (range: $0.01{-}10~\upmu\mathbf{M},$ ) towards ceftriaxone, ascribable to the synergistic effects of Pt NPs and MWCNTs. Likewise, carbon supported Pt nanoparticles were explored for detection of tetracycline antibiotics (Kushikawa et al. 2016). The response of the sensor was thoroughly investigated (in terms of an amount of Pt NPs and scan rate, electroactive surface area, and $\mathrm{pH}$ of the electrolyte) to measure the electrochemical response towards electro-oxidation of TET. Due to the good combination of activity and stability, Pt NPs/C composites had a favorable redox behavior  and reproducible electron transfer towards tetracycline oxidation. This sensor exhibited a wide detection range $(9.99{-}44.01\ \upmu\mathrm{M})$ and detection limit of $4.28~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ towards TET. These authors found the sensor to be promising for determining low TET concentration in urine samples, suggesting its enormous potential for clinical applications.
## 3.3.3 Magnetic nanomaterials
Magnetic nanomaterials (MNs) have been employed in diverse biosensing applications due to their inherent attributes such as controllable size, physico-chemical properties, large surface area, and low cost of production (Akbarzadeh et al. 2012; Reddy et al. 2012). Therefore, MNs possessing adequate physico-chemistry and tailored surface properties have been actively integrated into the transducer as immobilization supports for bio-recognition elements (e.g., antibodies, aptamers, MIPs, and peptides) to provide fast and specific detection of target analyte molecules. Further, the ease of chemical modification of MNs led to the development of novel multi-functional nanocomposites for a new generation electrochemical sensors (Xu et al. 2012; Rocha-Santos, T. A. et al. 2014). Based on the enormous potential of MNs, novel electroanalytical approaches have been investigated for the sensing of antibiotics in diverse matrices (e.g., human fluids, environmental, food, and beverage samples).
Initially antibodies were combined with MNs (as a recognition element) to develop electrochemical immunosensors for diverse antibiotics. In one such report (Yu et al.2013), nanocomposites of magnetic nanoparticles with silver metal nanoparticles and thionine functionalized graphene sheets (TH-GS/GA/Ag@Fe3O4NPs) were employed to construct a label-free electrochemical immunosensor for the sensing of kanamycin. Detailed SWV studies revealed a low DL $\mathrm{\Delta}[0.03~\mathrm{nM}$ over a linear range of $0.1{-}33~\mathrm{nM},$ ) towards kanamycin. These authors claimed that the excellent electroanalytical performance was a synergistic consequence of good electron transfer ability of graphene nanosheets and electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Ag}\ @\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocomposites. The sensor exhibited excellent sensitivity, high stability, and good selectivity (e.g., glucose, vitamin C, gentamicin, and neomycin), which ensured its applicability for kanamycin detection in animal derived food samples. Likewise, a gold electrode was modified with carboxyl $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles (using chitosan as a linker) to develop a low cost label-free electrochemical immunosensor for TET (Liu et al. 2016). Based on accelerated e- transfer kinetics and effective immobilization of Anti-TET monoclonal antibody on the electrode surface, the sensor demonstrated a detection limit of $0.07{\mathrm{~nM}}$ (linear range: 0.1–2.25 nM) towards tetracycline. The sensor response was tested towards major interferences (e.g., erythromycin, gentamycin, penicillin, and chloramphenicol) to evaluate it as a candidate for monitoring low-abundant antibiotics in food samples.
Further, the concept of an aptasensor was developed based on application of antibodies (as a biorecognition element  to antibiotics) for robust, cost-effective, and highly specific electrochemical sensing of antibiotics. In this respect, $\mathrm{IL}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were deposited onto a screen printed electrode for effective immobilization of anti-TET aptamer (Shi et al. 2017). Further, without any signal labels, the sensor showed a remarkable voltammetric electrochemical response with a DL of $1\ \mathrm{nM}$ (range: $1.0{-}10^{7}\ \mathrm{nM})$ towards tetracycline. This enhanced electroanalytical response was attributed to the interaction between $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles and IL, which facilitated electron transfer between the electrolyte/electrode interfaces. Further, the sensor showed excellent stability, reproducibility, and selectivity during real sample analysis of TET in milk samples.
To further improve the electrochemical response towards TET, an innovative concept of a magnetic bar carbon paste electrode was introduced (Jahanbani and Benvidi 2016). These authors modified the carbon paste electrode with magnetic bars for efficient accumulation of magnetic nanocomposites $(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}@{\bf O}_{\sf A})$ on the electrode surface. Detailed impedimetric analysis suggested a detection limit as low as 3.8 fM over a wide detection range $(0.01{-}10^{6}\mathrm{pM})$ , which was significantly improved from many previous studies for TET, e.g., with a DL of $1~\mathrm{{nM}}$ (Conzuelo et al. 2013; Zhang et al. 2014; Gan et al. 2014). The maximal adsorption of magnetic nanoparticles $\mathrm{(Fe_{3}O_{4}@O A)}$ (through magnetic bars present in the CPE) further resulted in excellent performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}@{\bf O}{\bf A}$ based sensors. The sensor was also found to exhibit excellent selectivity towards tetracycline in the presence of oxytetracycline and doxycycline antibiotics to promote real sample analysis in milk, honey, drug, and blood samples. Likewise, a novel signal amplification strategy was employed using nanocomposites of Au NP–functionalized magnetic multi-walled carbon nanotubes and nanoporous Pt-Ti alloy for sensitive electrochemical aptasensing of streptomycin (Yin et al. 2016). Here, the authors fabricated nanoporous Pt-Ti alloys using selective de-alloying Al from a PtTiAl alloy in a HCl solution. Based on the combined attributes, an LOD of $0.01\ \mathrm{nM}$ over a wide detection range $(0.00008{\scriptstyle-0.1\ \upmu\mathbf{M}})$ was realized using the DPV transduction method. The response of the electrode was also evaluated for interference, stability, and reproducibility to detect streptomycin in milk samples with high recoveries of $97.3\%-105.2\%$ . The potential of MNs was further extended towards multiplexed electrochemical detection of antibiotics using metal ion encoded magnetic hollow porous nanoparticles (MHPs) and ExoI assisted cascade multiple amplification (Yan et al. 2016). These authors chose oxytetracycline antibiotics (OTC) and chloramphenicol (CAP) as target models while MHPs served as soluble carriers for maximizing the immobilization of metal ions for signal enhancement. The employed dual signal amplification strategy resulted in a low detection limit of $0.4~\mathrm{nM}$ and $0.2{\mathrm{nM}}$ towards CAP and OTC, respectively, using the SWV technique. Further, the electrochemical response of the sensor was also found to be unaffected in the presence of structurally analogues antibiotics (e.g., doxycycline, tetracycline, kanamycin, and streptomycin), which made it a promising candidate for undergoing CAP and OTC detection in milk samples.
Recently, magnetic molecularly imprinted polymers (mMIP) have been conveniently applied for highly specific recognition of streptomycin antibiotics (Liu et al. 2013a). These authors synthesized mMIPs nanospheres using Au (III), which efficiently promoted molecularly imprinted polymerization with a streptomycin template on magnetic beads. The detection process was specifically designed to   execute a competitive–type assay involving competition between analyte molecules and glucose oxidase labeled streptomycin (GOx -STR) for molecular imprints upon the addition of streptomycin. A sensor based on the bioelectrocatalytic reaction induced a signal amplification strategy showing a linear detection range within the concentration range of 0.08–34.3 nM and an ultra-low DL of $0.01\ \mathrm{nM}$ towards STR. The sensing system had an excellent selectivity against chloramphenicol and tetracycline with recoveries between $81-129\%$ during streptomycin detection using real samples like milk and honey samples. Later, a highly specific, label free nanoenabled device was fabricated using magnetite nanoparticle modified MIPs (Fig. 5)(Alejandro Zamora-Gálvez et al. 2015). The sensor detected sulphonamides at a low detection limit of $1~\mathrm{pM}$ (concentration range: $0.1\mathrm{-}10^{7}\mathrm{nM})$ . The sensor was capable of sulphonamide analysis in spiked sea water samples without any potential interference from structurally analogues antibiotics (sulphadiazine and sulphacetamide).
Likewise, a kanamycin templated label-free MIP electrochemical sensor was developed using methacrylic acid (MAA) as a functional monomer (Long et al. 2015). The sensor could assay kanamycin with a detection limit of $23\ \mathrm{pM}$ (detection range: $0.0001{-}1.0~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . Further, kanamycin specific imprinted binding sites (possessing a complementary size and arrangement of functional groups specific to kanamycin) resulted in a sensor with high selectivity towards commonly employed antibiotics like streptomycin, gentamycin, and erythromycin. Further, in the presence of kanamycin imprinted binding sites, the sensing system exhibited excellent selectivity towards commonly employed structurally similar antibiotics (e.g., streptomycin, gentamycin, and erythromycin). In addition, magnetic nanostructures have also been conveniently employed to induce a direct electrocatalytic effect during antibiotic detection. In one such report (Ensafi et al. 2013), multiwalled $\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ decorated carbon nanotubes were explored as a mediator in the voltammetric detection of cefixime. This strategy is promising because no expensive bio-recognition element was employed in the detection procedure. The sensor exhibited a detection limit of $0.02\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (linear range: $0.1{-}600~\upmu\mathbf{M})$ towards cefixime using linear sweep voltammetry as a transduction method. The sensor also tolerated interferences from $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ , ${\mathrm{SO}_{4}}^{2\cdot}$ -, $\mathrm{CO}_{3}\mathrm{\cdot}$ , and tartaric acid, which confirmed its suitability for detection of cefixime in pharmaceutical tablets, urine, and plasma samples. Another similar type of investigation was done in which a PVP capped $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@(\$ Cd Se core shell modified electrode was introduced for the detection of rifampicin residues (trace amounts) in biological and pharmaceutical samples (Zeynali et al. 2017). The obtained excellent electrocatalytic activity (oxidation of rifampicin) was attributed to the large surface area, abundant active sites, and adsorptive capacity of PVP capped $\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Cd}$ Se core shell nanoparticles. A broad range of linearity was observed in the concentration range of $(0.0001{-}10^{5}\mathrm{pM})$ with a very low DL (0.04 fM) towards rifampicin. Using SWSV as a transduction method, the sensor revealed excellent selectivity towards common interferences (e.g., isoniazid, pyrazinamide, uric acid, and L-threonine) during rifampicin analysis in serum samples and pharmaceutical drugs. Recently, chitosan coated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles were incorporated in CPE to facilitate electron transfer associated with catalyzed redox processes of ciprofloxacin (Dehdashtian et al. 2016). Here, the sensor exhibited a DL of $0.01\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (a linear range: 6 $\upmu\mathrm{M}{-}75\upmu\mathrm{M})$ towards ciprofloxacin using DPV as a signal transduction method. The sensor exhibited good selectivity, high sensitivity, and acceptable recoveries (ranging within $97\%-105\%$ ) to undergo ciprofloxacin detection within serum and urine samples.
## 3.3.4. Metal-organic frameworks
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous coordination polymers with large internal pore volumes, well-defined pore-sizes, and high specific surface areas $(10,000\mathrm{~\textit~{~m~}~}^{2}\mathrm{~\textit~{~g~}~}^{1})$ (Kempahanumakkagari et al. 2018; Farha et al. 2012). Furthermore, MOFs can be built on an infinite number of structural combinations for diverse sensing applications due to their good tunability in pore size (ranging from angstroms to nanometers) and wide options for metal ions, organic linkers, and structural motifs (Kreno et al. 2012). Due to their inherent attributes, the potential of MOFs has been thoroughly investigated towards electrochemical sensing of antibiotics as discussed in the next section.
The application of molecularly imprinted polymer (MIP) microporous MOFs (MMOF) was proposed for the first time towards detection of tetracycline in honey samples (Bougrini et al. 2016). Here, imprinted composites were used for modifying the gold electrode surface through electropolymerization of p-amino-thiophenol functionalized gold nanoparticles with imprinted molecules. Further, the effects of various parameters (e.g., extraction time, incubation time, and washing and elution conditions) were thoroughly investigated to obtain a maximum level of electrochemical response towards tetracycline. The sensor demonstrated a DL of $0.22~\mathrm{fM}$ (concentration range: 224 fM to $22.4~\mathrm{nM}$ ) of tetracycline. Importantly, the sensor response remained unaffected in the presence of structural analogues (e.g. doxycycline), while showing high recoveries $(102\%-106\%$ ) for monitoring tetracycline in honey samples. Likewise, a chromium containing MOF (MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ ) was used for electrochemical sensing for chloramphenicol antibiotics (CAP) (Zhang et al. 2017). Considering the low conductivity of MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , high conductivity carbon black XC-72 was mixed with MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ to develop novel hybrid materials (MIL-101(Cr)/XC-72). Further, the authors investigated the effects of various parameters (e.g., MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ : XC 72, accumulation time, scan rate, and pH values) to obtain an optimum response for CAP detection. The sensor demonstrated a detection limit of $1.5\mathrm{nM}$ (range: $0.01{-}20\upmu\mathrm{M})$ ) towards CAP, which was far better than glassy carbon electrodes coated individually with MIL-101(Cr) or XC-72. Here, the high surface area of MIL101(Cr) worked with the excellent conductivity of XC-72 to yield an enhanced electrochemical response towards the detection of CAP. The sensor (MIL-101(Cr)/XC-72/GCE) response was tested for potential interferences (e.g., chlortetracycline, tetracycline, kiasamycin, kanamycin, penicillin, neomycin, gentamycin, and amikacin). This device was promising for the analysis of real samples (e.g., milk, honey, and eye drop). The successful application of MOFs was further extended towards multiplexed detection of antibiotics using metal ion doped nanoscale MOFs (signal tracers) and the Rec Jf exonuclease-catalyzed target recycling amplification process (Chen et al. 2016a). Here, the authors used capture beads (anti-ss DNA antibody [Ab] labeled on Dynabeads) and nanoscale MOF (NMOF) based signal tracers (metal ions and aptamers (specific to OTC and KAN [Apts-MNMs]) co-labeled with a nanoscale MOF $\mathrm{(UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ ) to fabricate aptasensors for multiplexed detection of antibiotic residues. Here, the authors employed a competitive replacement reaction between antibiotics and anti-ssDNA Ab with aptamers as a facile detection strategy, wherein the aptamers favorably bind to target antibiotics. Further, employment of Rec Jf exonuclease was found to liberate the target antibiotics (due to digestion of targets-Apts-MNM complexes) to produce more signal tracers detectable through a square wave voltammetric technique (Fig. 6). Due to the synergistic amplification effects exerted by NMOFs and Rec Jf exonuclease, the proposed electrochemical assay exhibited excellent sensitivity with detection limits of OTC and KAN of $0.18\ \mathrm{pM}$ and $0.15\ \mathrm{pM}$ , respectively. Apart from high sensitivity, the assay was almost unaffected by potential interferences (e.g., doxycycline [DOX], chloramphenicol [CAP], chlortetracycline [CTC], streptomycin [SM], and gentamicin sulfate [GS]), facilitating real sample analysis (e.g., milk). Later, this study group implemented aptamer-metal ion-NMOF-based electrochemical bio-codes for multiplexed sensing of CAP and KAN antibiotics (Chen et al. 2017a). In the bio-codes, a high surface area, porous amine functionalized NMOF (about $1{,}052~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ ) was used as a substrate to accommodate  numerous metal ions like ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ or $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ (capacities of $293.52~\mathrm{mg~g}^{-1}$ and $268.37~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively) for the signal amplification process. Further, as-synthesized signal tags, i.e., MNMOFs labeled with the complementary DNA strands of aptamers specific to KANA and CAP antibiotics, were later conjugated with aptamer functionalized magnetic beads (MB $@$ Apt 1/Apt 2) to obtain the bio-codes specific to KANA and CAP, respectively (Fig. 7). Here, rapid release of MNMOFs was programmed through the presence of magnetic beads (in response to the interactions between the aptasensor and target antibiotics) and detected by a square wave voltammetric transduction method. The application of bio-codes imparted excellent sensitivity (detection limit of $0.19\mathrm{p}\mathrm{\bfM}$ for CAP and $0.16\mathrm{pM}$ for KANA) over a range of $0.7\mathrm{-}49.0\upmu\mathrm{M}$ for both targets. Further, this method was not affected by any potential interference from structurally analogues antibiotics (e.g., oxytetracycline, chlorotetracycline, $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Mg}^{+2}$ ). As such, the proposed electrochemical assay was implemented successfully for the detection of CAP and KANA in milk samples.
The same study group further employed Y-DNA probe-based metal ion encoded nanoscale metal organic framework (NMOF) substrates and a circular strand-replacement DNA polymerization (CSRP) based amplification strategy for multiplexed detection of chloramphenicol (CAP) and oxytetracycline (OTC) (Chen et al. 2017b). Here, Y-DNA probes were assembled using an assisted DNA probe (labeled with magnetic Au NPs) to be hybridized with the captured DNA probe (consisting of aptamer and primer recognition region) and signal tags (NMOF encapsulating signal DNAs and different metal ions like $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ or $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ). The NMOF was found to provide a large specific area substrate for efficient loading of numerous metal ions to produce prominent electrochemical signals. Moreover, the high affinity of target antibiotics (compared with complementary sequences) towards the aptamer domain efficiently induced a conformational change within the captured DNA. This phenomenon eventually facilitated the release of signal tags, which were further amplified by the cyclic-induced polymerization through the assistance of Bst DNA polymerase (CSRP). Accordingly, the sensor resulted in 17-fold amplification of electrochemical signals to yield OTC and CAP detection limits of 48 and 33 fM, respectively. Further, it exhibited an excellent selectivity towards CAP and OTC in the presence of potential interferences including proteins (e.g., $a-$ lactalbumin [α-LA], casein [CAS], and $\upbeta$ -lactoglobulin $[\upbeta$ -LG]), common metal ions (e.g., $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{K}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ), and various antibiotics (e.g., doxycycline [DOX], kanamycin [KAN], tetracycline [TET], and gentamicin sulfate [GS]) that are commonly present in milk. Moreover, the proposed assay was universally applicable for analysis of food safety by the addition of metal ions for the signal probe or substitution of appropriate aptamers. Likewise, a novel ultrasensitive electrochemical assay was developed for multiplexed sensing of
CAP and KAN using a dual recycling amplification strategy based on endonuclease and exonuclease (Huang et al. 2018). Using the square wave voltammetric transduction method, a linear correspondence was observed between the current intensities and concentration over a wide detection range for both KANA and CAP $(10^{-4}-50~\mathrm{nM})$ ). Here, HP-UIO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was employed for effective encapsulation of signal tags while gold nanoparticles further enriched and amplified EcoRI and Bam HI restriction endonucleases. In the presence of targets (KANA and CAP antibiotics), numerous signal tags were diffused into the reaction solution to achieve 54 fold amplification of signal towards KANA and CAP at detection limits of 35 and 21 fM, respectively. These authors observed that the electrochemical response of the sensor was insignificant towards structural analogues antibiotics (e.g., florphenicol [FF], tetracycline [TTC], thiamphenicol [TAP], oxytetracycline [OTC], and erythromycin [ETC]), when detecting KANA and CAP in fish samples. Additionally, the proposed method provided results comparable to ELISA, suggesting that it is promising for the sensing of multiplexed antibiotics in food samples.
Recently, nanohybrids of COF and Ce-based MOF (Ce-MOF@COF) were built as label-free biosensing platforms for electrochemical detection of oxytetracycline (OTC) for the first time (Zhou et al. 2019). The sensor exhibited a linear electrochemical response towards OTC (concentration range: $0.2\mathrm{~-~}1.0\mathrm{nM})$ with a detection limit of 35fM. This enhanced performance of nanohybrids can be ascribed to the exceptional electrochemical performance, aqueous dispersion ability, and strong bio-affinity towards OTC-specific aptamers. Additionally, the excellent analytical performance of the aptasensor was further combined with  great reproducibility, high selectivity, stability, and sufficient practical applicability for detecting OTC in real samples (e.g., milk, waste water, and urine samples).
## 3.3.5 Quantum Dots
QDs are well-known candidates for diverse bio-sensing applications due to their many well-known merits (e.g., inherent high aspect ratio, excellent biocompatibility, good interfacial properties, high electron-transfer efficiency, and feasibility for surface modification) (Li et al. 2013; Liu et al. 2007; Khene et al. 2011). During multiplexed electrochemical biosensing applications, QDs act as signal tags by exploiting the oxidation potentials of the associated metal ions (Wang et al. 2003; Vijian et al. 2016). Despite their enormous potential, their bio-sensing applications also suffer from the high toxicity of heavy metal based QDs, long and laborious synthetic procedures, lack of reproducibility in size, and possibility of agglomeration (Pedrero et al. 2017). Therefore, this section (3.3.5) was organized to summarize the recent advancements of QD-based electrochemical sensing of antibiotics.
A facile electrochemical sensing approach was proposed for the detection of ciprofloxacin (CIP) based on complex formation between in-situ generated Cd (II) ions and CIP from CdS QDs (Shan et al. 2016). The sensor exhibited superior detectability for CIP with a DL of $22{\mathrm{nM}}$ . However, with this sensor design, excellent selectivity of CIP was also observed against interfering components (e.g., ofloxacin, chloramphenicol, kanamycin, erythromycin, and gentamicin). In addition, its suitability for CIP analysis was further supported by acceptable recoveries ranging from $99.1\%-109.6\%$ in human urine samples. To overcome the toxicity associated with CdS quantum dots, an advanced strategy was developed for the voltammetric determination of levofloxacin (LEV) using composites of poly(o-aminophenol) and GQDs fabricated by electropolymerization (Huang et al. 2017). It was observed that excellent electrical conductivity and electron transfer efficiency of GQDs enabled an ultra-low DL of $10~\mathrm{{nM}}$ towards LEV. The response of the sensor was tested in the presence of four structurally similar analogues to LEV (e.g., ciprofloxacin, enrofloxacin, lomefloxacin, and norfloxacin) with enhanced recoveries of $96.0\substack{-101\%}$ , which support its applicability for LEV detection within spiked milk samples. The potential of QDs was further supported by ultra-sensitive multiplexed assays of antibiotics with inexpensive instrumentation (Chen et al. 2016b). In this context, Chen and co-workers developed a reliable strategy using magnetic aptamer–modified
QD encoded dendritic nanotracer-based voltammetric assay for the determination of chloramphenicpol (CAP) antibiotic in the presence of persistent organic pollutant polychlorinated biphenyl-72  (PCB 72:5). Here, amplification features of the dendritic nanotracers enabled a Dl of 1.0 pM towards CAP, which was significantly lower than those obtained for commercially available ELISA kits. In addition to high sensitivity, the assay exhibited excellent specificity against similar structure compounds like kanamycin (KAN), oxytetracycline (OTC), PCB 101, and PCB72. Moreover, the sensor recorded acceptable recoveries (ranging from $92.0\%-102.1\%)$ ) to screen toxic antibiotic residues in food and environment samples. Further, a sensitive detection strategy was proposed for determination of major antibiotic pollutants (streptomycin [STR], chloramphenicol [CHL], and tetracycline [TET]) together using aptamers and QD-based tags (Fig. 8) (Xue at al. 2016). These authors explored the effects of various parameters (e.g., concentration of Ap-DNAs/cDNA1s, cDNA2s used to modify QDs, and capture-DNAs along with the incubation time of antibiotics) to acquire the maximum response toward the target antibiotics. The optimal synergy (among aptamerbased hybridization and replacement, electrochemical techniques, and inherent signal amplification features) enabled ultra-low detection limits for STR $\mathrm{i}0\mathrm{nM})$ , CHL $(5~\mathrm{nM})$ , and TET $(20~\mathrm{nM})$ in milk samples.
## 4. Performance evaluation of different options
The potential utility of electrochemical sensors has been recognized due to their inherent sensitivity, selectivity, cost-effectiveness, and ease of use. However, the performance of such sensors relies significantly on the choice of materials, transduction method, and type of antibiotics employed in the detection process. The performance of nanomaterial-based electrochemical sensors towards antibiotic detection is thus compared with respect to different parameters of quality assurance (e.g.,
LOD, linear range, sensitivity, and selectivity) for diverse antibiotic contaminants.   Accordingly, MWCNT modified electrochemical sensor (anti-TET/MWCNTs/GCE) exhibited high sensitivities towards tetracycline with an LOD of $5\mathrm{nM}$ (Table 3). The performance of this system was improved further to a value of $0.005{\mathrm{~nM}}$ using an electrode modified with nanocomposites of chitosan-Prussian blue-graphene and multiwalled carbon nanotubes (Anti-TET/Gr/(CS-PB-GR)2/MWCNTs-CS/GCE). Further, Au NPs modified with MB (MB/Au NPs) demonstrated a comparable limit of detection of 4 pM, which was significantly improved using magnetic nanoparticles $(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\ \mathrm{NPs}@{\mathrm{OA}})$ to obtain a DL of 3 fM towards tetracycline. However, the presence of well-defined pore-sizes and high specific surface area allowed MMOF-MIP to demonstrate an excellent limit of detection (0.22 fM) towards tetracycline. In this way, MMOF-MIP is considered as a superior candidate for performing electrochemical sensing of tetracycline as compared to the existing “state of the art” nanomaterials employed for the same purpose. However, PtNPs exhibited a relatively high detection limit (4,280 nM) towards tetracycline. As such, this result suggested that noble metal nanoparticles should be modified further to act as a sensitive material for the detection of tetracycline.
Further, carbon nanomaterials served as a cost-effective alternative with exceptional physicochemical properties to undergo electrochemical sensing of chloramphenicol (Table 3). Composites of MWCNT (MWCNT-CTAB-PDPA) had a comparable limit of detection $(2{\mathrm{~nM}})$ ) to that of MIL-101(Cr)/XC-7, which were successful in achieving an ultra-low detection limit $(1.5~\mathrm{{nM})}$ towards chloramphenicol detection. Similar to tetracycline, diverse nanomaterials (carbonaceous nanomaterials (e.g., graphene), metal nanoparticles, magnetic nanomaterials, and metal organic frameworks) have been employed for electrochemical sensing of kanamycin (Table 3).  In this regard, an electrode modified with a composite film of water-soluble graphene sheet (WGS)/Prussian bluechitosan (PB-CTS)/nanoporous gold (NPG) (Anti-Kan/WGS/PB-CTS/NPG) achieved an LOD of
0.013 nM towards kanamycin. This method was improved further to a DL value of $0.8\ \mathrm{pM}$ with successful application of thionine functionalized graphene (GR-TH) and hierarchical nanoporous (HNP) PtCu alloy as biosensing substrates (Aptamer/HNP-PtCu/GR-TH/GCE). However, NMOFbased biocodes demonstrated a lower detection limit $(0.00016~\mathrm{nM})$ towards kanamycin, which was significantly lower than that of other nanomaterial-based counterparts. Thus, based on the electrochemical response, functionalized graphene is an efficient candidate for performing electrochemical sensing of kanamycin due to the ease of functionalization (with metal nanoparticles, aptamers, and antibodies) and the large surface area. However, in comparison to graphene, NMOFbased biocodes seem to be more sensitive as advanced electrode materials for the detection of kanamycin antibiotics $\mathrm{\Phi(DL\0.16\pM)}$ in diverse sensing matrices. Finally, Table 3  enlists four different types of nanomaterial based transducers for electrochemical sensing of streptomycin. All nanomaterials imparted a low LOD with a wide linear range along with many other merits (e.g., excellent reproducibility, high sensitivity, and good stability) for measuring streptomycin in diverse (e.g., honey, eye drop, milk, urine, and water) samples. Among all, NP-PtTi/Au@MWCNTs–Fe3O4 exhibited a DL of $0.01\ \mathrm{nM}$ , which was significantly lower than any other transducer materials reported for streptomycin detection to date.
A closer comparison of performance between nanomaterials-assisted sensing systems for antibiotics clearly indicates that nanomaterials serve a crucial role in improvising the sensitivity as demonstrated from the lowest detection limit towards the antibiotics detection. The highly porous nature of MOFs allowed MMOF-MIP and NMOF-based biocodes to achieve ultra–low detection limit towards tetracycline and kanamycin, respectively. Similarly, porous composites of carbonaceous nanomaterials are well suited to undergo sensitive detection of chloramphenicol. Therefore, well-defined porous nature, high surface area, and structure of antibiotics are considered as key parameters for developing electrode transducers for ultra-sensitive detection for antibiotics. In addition, extended efforts are in great demand to establish for facile functionalization technique so that one can fabricate multi-component composites to achieve ultra-low detection limit towards trace antibiotics residues. In this regard, NP-PtTi/Au@MWCNTs–Fe $\phantom{0}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ composites are also recognized as a suitable example where an ultra-low detection limit $\left(0.01{\mathrm{~nM}}\right)$ was achieved towards streptomycin, which was significantly lower than any other transducer employed for its detection.
## 5.  Conclusion and future perspectives
The present review was organized to describe recent advancements in the field of nanostructured materials for improving the performance of electrochemical sensors (e.g., sensitivity, selectivity, and dynamic range) towards quantitative detection of antibiotics in diverse matrices. Electrochemical sensing methods built with the aid of nanomaterial technologies are demonstrated to have enhanced performance with rapid, accurate, and senstive detection of antibiotics. A detailed comparative analysis among various nanostructured materials has confirmed that the sensitivity of the sensor can be improved significantly through various routes (e.g., chemical functionalization, hybrid composite fabrication, and modulation of surface architecture of these nanomaterials). Nonetheless, further development in this research field must be made to be able to address the major hurdles in this research field, which include: i) controlled synthesis (in terms of size, shape, and surface area), ii) design of novel functional  nanometric interfaces,  iii) understanding the  relationships between structure, composition, and reactivity of nanomaterials, iv) biocompatibility of nanomaterials with the biorecognition molecules,  and  v) cross interference within the structurally similar antibiotics (e.g., oxytetracycline, tetracycline, and chlotetracycline). As such, the use of NM-based electrochemical sensing systems are expected to offer highly reliable on-site analysis of antibiotics in complex biological environments as well as real world conditions. The technical challenges associated with this advanced sensing tools can be resolved further by achieving synergy between multifunctional nanomaterials, recognition elements, and electrochemical methods. Furthermore, the desirable portable devices may be developed through efficient connectivity and integration of electrochemical sensors using communication devices (e.g., smartphones and tablets). It is assumed that an efficient integration between two distinct areas of research might have great potential to effectively revolutionize all available options for antibiotic diagnostics.
